Phase 6 qualification inputs (2026-10-17)
  User-run visual acceptance
    The user reports: "I have already verified ingame appearence as fine."
    This records general in-game appearance acceptance; no unreported per-mode or scene-matrix
      observations are inferred. Further performance improvements are a separate requested discussion,
      not permission to change the approved rendering behavior during qualification.
  Performance target and evidence
    The user clarified: "arent discussing a performance target right now, jsut verify that its faster".
      Local hardware discovery identifies an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090 (driver 610.88, 24,564 MiB);
      an actual GL timing run must also record its GL renderer/version to rule out a software adapter.
      No game launch, deployment, measured speedup or performance acceptance is implied by discovery.
    Task -> source -> evidence: target/cost matrix -> proposal Acceptance and this checklist Execution
      conventions / Phase 6 -> matched per-generation construction plus production reduced transport
      and reconstruction timings are required, including sparse/dense/empty/separated maps, view
      positions, animated inputs/padded edges and allocation reuse. Existing correctness/probe-count
      receipts do not establish that benefit. Acceptance uses relative total GPU costs; no absolute millisecond budget is required.
  Saved-setting observation and timing readiness
    Read-only clientsettings.json inspection reports screenWidth=1846, screenHeight=980, ssaa=1,
      cloudRenderMode=1 and viewDistance=1536. These are saved preferences, not a verified active
      framebuffer or an agreed qualification target. That view distance implies the native 200-wide
      maximum control map under the inspected producer formula. Saved VGE configuration contains
      no explicit cloud controls; actual running quality must be captured rather than inferred.
    Existing GpuTimerQuery and GpuTimestampQuery can measure completed GPU work. Production
      GraphicsCommandContext has identifiable BeginPass/EndPass boundaries for reduced trace,
      temporal filtering and resolve; test-only observation can separate these without changing
      production sampling or adding runtime profiling policy. Total timing must also enclose
      per-generation CloudHierarchy.TryBuild and production CloudVolumeReconstruction.Draw.
    The saved 1846x980 extent was selected initially, not asserted as a live framebuffer.
      The full matrix uses 129x73 after scaling pilots exposed expensive shader workloads; larger
      extent controls are recorded separately rather than implied by the bounded matrix. No benchmark or regression
      result is claimed from these saved settings or source inspection.
  Relative-cost qualification method
    User instruction supersedes the earlier request for an absolute budget. The chosen comparison
      is RTX 4090, bounded 129x73 full-matrix extent, explicit synthetic 75-degree vertical field of view,
      default CloudVolumeSettings and half-width/half-height tracing. No absolute speed target or
      live framebuffer dimensions are inferred. Original means same-candidate DDA with summaries
      unavailable; accelerated means certified summaries rebuilt for each native generation.
    Phase 6 measurement -> proposal Acceptance / Ownership and publication; inherited volumetric
      proposal Quality and temporal behavior / Publication and native lifecycle -> opt-in
      CloudHierarchyPerformanceTests, CloudPerformanceScene and CloudPerformanceTimer. Both owners
      execute actual CloudVolumeReconstruction.Draw. Equal timestamp observation counts measure
      construction, trace, temporal and resolve; a separate outer interval covers construction and
      complete production preparation/submission/restoration, rather than summing stage medians.
      CPU submission is recorded separately from query-result waits. Paired inputs and PMJ frames
      are identical, ordering alternates and first-use work is warmed outside recorded samples.
    Matrix: 200-wide empty/sparse/dense/separated-height maps from ground/inside/above, plus odd
      81-wide sparse/separated native-edge views. Native bounds animate each generation; solar/lunar
      directions update periodically while matching completed lighting can retain temporal history.
      Stable allocation identities and owned texture payload are recorded; payload is not residency.
    Common retained native map production and scene-HZB work are outside both measured VGE paths;
      synthetic uploads, neutral OIT clears, first-use allocation/compilation, output verification
      readbacks and query waits are excluded identically. These controlled costs qualify relative
      VGE cloud work, not complete game-frame timing. Selected output comparisons remain outside
      timing. No runtime rendering algorithm changes or further performance optimization is included.

  Measured relative costs (2026-10-17)
    Candidate: canonical Debug build (not Release or live whole-frame qualification); production DLL SHA256
      D88A182FC5DC078C3A3B93C19B1F903EA757130129DFDB958FB1D0FB598A6909.
      SPIR-V uses the build tool's fixed -O optimization; Debug also emits -g metadata.
      The three new files are opt-in benchmark fixtures only; inherited cloud runtime, shader and test
      sources match the Phase 5 manifest without drift. Build passed 458/537 production/test shader
      variants with 0 errors and 164 existing recompilation warnings. Existing production correctness
      and packaging receipts remain applicable to unchanged source; no algorithm was modified.
    Adapter: GL renderer NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090/PCIe/SSE2, OpenGL 4.3.0 NVIDIA 610.88; hardware
      CSVs record P0 and clocks/temperature before and after. No game process was observed. Host,
      raw timing arrays, actual settings, lighting cadence, allocation identities and build hash are
      recorded with each measured scene. These are controlled synthetic workloads, not native captures.
    Final full matrix: 129x73, 28 scenes across both PBR selections, 24 paired frames after four
      warmups, 672 pairs total; two xUnit theories passed in 5m35s with no failures/skips. Timed
      committed history agrees in all seven channels, selected reconstructed OIT agrees in all six,
      nonempty scenes contain cloud pixels and allocations remain stable. Each scene records 21/24
      compatible history frames; lighting publication changes account for the other three.
      A 513x273 dense-inside scale pilot passed both modes; a 1846x980 empty-ground control passed
      both modes with 24 pairs each. The full nonempty matrix was not run at saved resolution.
    Median total GPU costs, including fresh hierarchy construction (standalone PBR; PBR+LumOn
      selection shows the same direction of change):
      129x73 dense/ground: 88.443 ms DDA -> 65.784 ms hierarchy, 25.6% faster.
      129x73 dense/above: 60.351 ms -> 34.325 ms, 43.1% faster.
      129x73 dense/inside: 86.004 ms -> 143.063 ms, 66.3% slower.
      129x73 sparse/inside: regression in both selections, approximately 20% slower.
      1846x980 empty/ground: 4.307 ms -> 0.561 ms, approximately 87% faster; all 24 pairs win.
      Dense-inside regression also persists at 513x273 (approximately 73 ms -> 132 ms).
      Construction is approximately 0.03 ms on 200-wide maps; the significant regression is in
      tracing, not reduction or unchanged temporal/resolve passes. CPU submission is recorded
      separately and is much smaller than those GPU trace costs.
    Separated-height 550-unit gaps are deliberately non-native shader-domain stress. The odd81
      edge camera is 25 units inside the upper native boundary, not normal native camera-following
      placement. Their regressions are retained in raw results, not presented as observed weather.
      No single aggregate speedup is claimed across differently weighted scenes or resolutions.
    Owned payload: 174,760 bytes hierarchy for width200; 43,688 bytes for width81. Reconstruction
      payload per owner is 692,640 bytes at129x73 and130,253,760 bytes at1846x980, plus512 PMJ bytes.
      Both original and accelerated retain the same reconstruction allocation; hierarchy is extra.
      These are nominal texel payloads, not measured resident GPU memory or allocator overhead.
    Evidence: artifacts/CloudHierarchy/phase6-result-summary.json contains all 32 final matrix/scale/
      full-size-control records, stage medians, paired distributions and conditional empirical-frame
      bootstrap intervals. Raw phase6-measurements, phase6-scale-pilot and phase6-full-size-control
      directories and matching TRX/logs retain the observations. Smoke/pilot receipts are separate.
      phase6-candidate-hashes.json records 90 inputs/binaries; phase6-candidate-recheck.json reports
      zero drift. Exact commands: phase6-commands.json. The 53-receipt identity manifest is
      phase6-receipt-hashes.json; no production or shader source changed during measurement.
    Verdict: the hierarchy is faster for some tested views, but the unqualified relative-speed claim
      is contradicted by relevant dense/sparse inside-cloud cases. Performance acceptance and Phase 6
      completion remain open; regressions require the requested separate optimization discussion.
      User appearance approval remains recorded. Detailed unreported live mode/scenario coverage and
      whole-frame timing are not inferred. No game launch, deployment or optimization occurred.
    Second review and independent linked-document audit verified the declared measurement work,
      all 90 candidate hashes and all 53 receipt identities without drift. The first two Phase 6
      tasks are satisfied for the named extents/workloads; they do not establish universal speedup,
      full-resolution nonempty-scene costs or unreported detailed live coverage. The audit does not
      pass Phase 6 completion: inside-cloud regressions remain unresolved pending optimization
      discussion. General user appearance acceptance is retained without asking for a repeat.

